  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1210 AM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed; the ridge that has been in \nplace over the Four Corners region is still likely to stay \nnearly stationary over the next week. By Saturday, guidance \nshows a signal that the ridge will weaken as a trough moves \nalong the U.S./Canada border. Throughout the week, there should \nbe a few shortwaves moving through the area.\n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to \"cool\" down to the mid \nto high 90s. Winds look to remain under 25 MPH with RH values \nstaying above 25%, minimizing fire weather concerns.\n\nTuesday's PoPs are currently forecast to be in the 30-60% \nrange, with the highest chances in eastern Colorado. Guidance \nindicates that the surface low to the south of the CWA will \nlikely remain there throughout the day. With the low stationary,\nwinds should mainly be from the east-northeast, promoting \nupslope flow for showers and storms. After looking at the \ninstability and shear for severe storm potential, there is a low\nend chance that they would be severe, something we will need to\nwatch over the next few days. For the northwestern portions of \nthe CWA, there is a 25-40% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of \nprecipitation from Tuesday afternoon into early Wednesday \nmorning.\n\nWednesday still look to have higher precipitation chances of \nthe long-term period as a shortwave propagates through during \nthe afternoon and evening hours. Guidance indicates a boundary \nmoving in from the northwest toward the southeast, though models\ndisagree on its exact location and timing. Currently, both the \nGFS and Canadian models show the boundary. However, the GFS \nbrings it in during the late afternoon hour, whereas the \nCanadian delays it until early Thursday morning. Other guidance \ndoesn't have the boundary. If it does materialize, the severe \nstorm potential is again low, but not 0. PoPs are forecast in \nthe 40-65 range, giving eastern Colorado the highest PoPs. \nEastern Colorado also has a 25-45% chance of seeing 0.10\" of \nprecipitation. Give this is roughly four days out, it will be \ninteresting to see how guidance trends for the location and \nspeed of the boundary.\n\nFor the rest of the week and weekend, PoPs steadily decrease. \nThursday's chances are around 20-30% while Friday-Sunday stay \naround 10-20%. However, with shortwaves passing through, any \nfavorable lifting mechanism could increase the precipitation \nchances.",
